<h1>list</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A collection of items accessible one after another beginning at the <a href="head.html"><em>head</em></a> and ending at the <a href="tail.html"><em>tail</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="bag.html"><em>bag</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="linkedList.html"><em>linked list</em></a>, <a href="doublyLinkedList.html"><em>doubly linked list</em></a>, <a href="circularlist.html"><em>circular list</em></a>, <a href="selfOrganizingList.html"><em>self-organizing list</em></a>, <a href="orderedLinkedList.html"><em>ordered linked list</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="orthogonalLists.html"><em>orthogonal lists</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A list may have additional access methods.  A list may be ordered.  A list can be seen as an ordered <a href="bag.html"><em>bag</em></a>.  A list may be kept as the leading items of an <a href="array.html"><em>array</em></a>, but inserting items other than at the end takes time.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

<hr>

